<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Trek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a>–1840s</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Trek was the migration of Dutch-speaking settlers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> into the interior of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from 1835. Some 12,000 to 15,000 people left, resenting British rule, the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and insecurity on the frontier. The trekkers fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics of Natalia, the Orange Free State and the South African Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-trek.jpg" alt="Great Trek" width="320" height="274" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Discott · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Trek_map_full.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> occupied the Cape by force in the early 19th century and formally assumed permanent control around 1815. Relations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> farmers and the new administration quickly soured. British authorities opposed the Boers' ownership of slaves and what they saw as harsh treatment of indigenous peoples, and insisted that the colony finance itself through taxation. In 1815 the Slachter's Nek Rebellion ended with five Boers executed for insurrection. In 1828 the governor gave all native inhabitants except slaves the rights of citizens, and English replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in the courts.</p><p>The abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1834 amplified the resentment. Some 35,000 registered slaves were to be freed, and compensation had to be collected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, which few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> could afford. Some considered leaving for the unmapped interior, and trekboers were frustrated that the British would not extend the colony's borders eastward. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church">Dutch Reformed Church</a> refused to endorse the migration, and many Boers stayed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1834–1835 a Kommissietrek led by Petrus Lafras Uys scouted Natal, and Jan Gerritze Bantjes's report on Natalia helped inspire the migration. The first wave, from 1835 to 1840, involved an estimated 6,000 people, and the first two parties, under Louis Tregardt and Hans van Rensburg, left in September 1835. In October 1836 an army of 4,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> attacked Hendrik Potgieter's laager at Vegkop and was repulsed by thirty-five armed trekkers. Punitive raids then forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a> and his followers to flee to what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>.</p><p>In October 1837 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-retief">Piet Retief</a> met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> to negotiate for land, and Dingane had Retief and seventy followers killed. Zulu forces then attacked encampments at Blaauwkrans and Weenen, killing 532 people, including 282 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a>. On 16 December 1838 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a> defeated a Zulu army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blood-river">Battle of Blood River</a>. Dingane's half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a> allied with the trekkers to overthrow him, and the Voortrekkers proclaimed the Natalia Republic, which the British annexed in 1843.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Great Trek led directly to the founding of autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics, the South African Republic, the Orange Free State and Natalia, and to conflicts that displaced the Northern Ndebele and contributed to the decline and collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a>. The trek broke down the earlier class hierarchy, and leaders relied on patriarchal family structure and military reputation to keep control, with lasting effects on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> culture.</p><p>The centenary of 1938 played a major role in the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> nationalism. It included a re-enactment ending at the Voortrekker Monument in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, completed in 1949. 16 December, first called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>'s Day and later the Day of the Vow, was a public holiday, renamed the Day of Reconciliation after apartheid.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Trek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British occupation, the abolition of slavery and the Khoikhoi reforms helped provoke the <b>Great Trek</b>, in which several thousand Dutch-speaking farmers left the colony from 1835 to found republics in Natal, the Orange Free State and the Transvaal, the origin of the later conflict between Afrikaners and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>One verse pledges dedication to the Afrikaners (left out of the English version) and another recalls the <b>Great Trek</b> of the Voortrekkers; the fourth verse, added at the government's request, is religious.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-stem-van-suid-afrika">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</a></li></ul></section>
